course: dictionary
lesson: 11988
title: Cf: compare this
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words in books are very short and are not meant to be read aloud. «cf.» is one of them. It is short for the Latin word «confer», which means «compare». When you see it, the writer is asking you to look at something else and compare it with what you have just read.
explain: Read this example: «The tiger's stripes are bold (cf. the zebra's stripes on page 14).» The writer wants you to turn to page 14 and think about how the zebra's stripes are similar to or different from the tiger's. You do not have to agree with anything. You just look and compare.
explain: You will mostly find «cf.» in dictionaries, textbooks and notes, often inside brackets. It is written only. When you read it, say «compare» in your head, and you will know exactly what to do.
fact: «cf.» is short for the Latin word «confer», which means «compare».
fact: It is written only: people say «compare» out loud, not «cf».
fact: It tells the reader to look at another example that is similar or different in a useful way.
fact: You will mostly see «cf.» in reference books such as textbooks and dictionaries, often inside brackets.
fact: «The river is long (cf. the map).» means: look at the map and compare it with the river.
exercise: What does «cf.» mean? | *Compare | Confuse | Carry | Copy
exercise: «Dogs are loyal (cf. cats, page 20).» What should you do with the cats on page 20? | Ignore them completely | Turn them into a question | Read them out loud as «cf» | *Compare them with dogs
exercise: Which of these is short for the Latin word «confer»? | etc. | *cf. | e.g. | p.m.
test: «Our school is old (cf. the castle, page 5).» Why does the writer write «cf.»? | To tell you the castle is closed | *To ask you to compare the school with the castle | To show the school is new | To say the school is wrong
test: How do people say «cf.» out loud? | *They say «compare» | They never say anything at all | They say «confer» in every sentence | They say «see eff»
exam: «Read the map (cf. the table on page 3) and write one way they are different.» What does «cf.» ask you to do? | Skip the map and read on | Copy the word map | Read the table out loud as a sentence | *Compare the map with the table
